course: dictionary
lesson: 2797
title: Out of proportion
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Disproportionate comes from proportion, which means how big one thing is compared with another. When something is disproportionate, it is not in a fair or sensible balance with the thing it belongs to. It is too large or too small for what it is about.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «A disproportionate punishment» is a punishment that does not fit the wrong that was done. If a child is kept in for a week for one small mistake, the punishment is too big for the wrong. So disproportionate means out of balance, not just big.
explain: Check with a swap. Put «too big for the job» into the sentence in place of the hard word. If «a too big for the job punishment» still makes sense as the meaning, you have the right idea. The word is a fair way to describe something that is not in fair balance.
fact: Disproportionate means too large or too small compared with something else.
fact: Something is disproportionate when it is not in a fair or sensible proportion.
fact: «A disproportionate punishment» is one that is too harsh for the wrong it answers.
fact: «Disproportionately large» means large in a way that is out of balance with the rest.
fact: In maths, a disproportionate share is more or less than the fair share, and in drawing, a head too big for the body has disproportionate parts.
exercise: «A threat far bigger than the thing it is about is a disproportionate threat.» What does disproportionate mean here? | Hidden under the ground | Exactly the right size for the job | *Too big compared with the thing it is about | Very quick to arrive
exercise: A child draws a head much bigger than the body. What is wrong with the drawing? | It is in colour | *Its parts are disproportionate | It is the wrong way up | It has no head
exercise: Four friends should share 8 sweets fairly, but Max takes 5. What word describes his share? | Fair | Equal | Tiny | *Disproportionate
test: Which word is closest in meaning to disproportionate? | Equal | *Out of balance | Fair | Balanced
test: «A disproportionate amount of time» means the time is what, compared with what it should be? | Just right | *More or less than it should be, compared with something else | Shared equally | Hidden
exam: Which use of disproportionate fits its meaning? | A sunny day in the park | A friend who lives nearby | A cup of tea that is hot | *A punishment far bigger than the wrong it answers
